  "prompt_text": "The figure compares several modular multilevel inverter topologies for generating three-phase outputs \\(V_a\\), \\(V_b\\), and \\(V_c\\) from DC sources. Across all panels, repeated shaded blocks indicate stacked converter cells used in each phase leg to synthesize multilevel AC waveforms.\n\nThe first two schematics show single-switching-cell approaches. One uses a half-bridge submodule with a capacitor and two switches, while the other uses a neutral-point-clamped style cell with four switches, two capacitors, and diodes, suggesting different ways to create stepped voltage levels from the same DC link.\n\nThe larger central diagram focuses on a generic “SM” submodule and shows two possible internal implementations: a four-switch full bridge or a simpler two-switch variant. Many of these submodules are connected in series in upper and lower arms, each arm including an inductor, which is characteristic of a modular multilevel converter structure.\n\nThe rightmost diagram presents a cascaded H-bridge arrangement. Here, separate isolated DC sources feed individual H-bridge cells labeled HB1 to HB\\(n\\), which are stacked to build the phase voltage. Overall, the figure emphasizes how different cell designs and energy-source arrangements can realize multilevel three-phase power conversion.",
